{1 Hilite}

{! Hilite} is a library for syntax highlighting using textmate grammars.

{2:quick_start Quick start}

By default, {! Hilite} comes with a few pre-packaged grammars.

{@ocaml[
# Hilite.langs;;
- : string list =
["ocaml"; "dune"; "opam"; "sh"; "shell"; "diff"; "bash"; "python"; "go"]
]}

If you are highlighting one of these languages, then you can use {! Hilite.src_code_to_html}
to produce HTML ready for styling.

{@ocaml[
# Hilite.src_code_to_html ~lang:"sh" "echo hello | rev";;
- : (string, Hilite.error) result =
Ok
 "<pre><code><span class='sh-support-function-builtin'>echo</span><span class='sh-source'> hello </span><span class='sh-keyword-operator-pipe'>|</span><span class='sh-source'> rev\n</span></code></pre>"
]}

Alternatively, if you are targetting something that isn't HTML, you can use the
lower-level {! Hilite.src_code_to_pairs} to receive the token alongside its
classification according to the grammar.

{@ocaml[
# Hilite.src_code_to_pairs ~lang:"sh" "echo hello | rev";;
- : ((string * string) list list, [> `Unknown_lang of string ]) result =
Ok
 [[("sh-support-function-builtin", "echo"); ("sh-source", " hello ");
   ("sh-keyword-operator-pipe", "|"); ("sh-source", " rev\n")]]
]}

{2:markdown Markdown support}

Consult {! Hilite_markdown}.

{2:customisation Customisation}

{! Hilite} supports users loading their own grammars so they can do custom,
build-time syntax highlighting. Users most provided a collection of textmate
grammars to the transformation function.

Here we will use a predefined grammar that comes along with the `Hilite`
library.

{@ocaml[
# let tm =
    let t = TmLanguage.create () in
    let grammar = TmLanguage.of_yojson_exn Hilite.Grammars.diff in
    TmLanguage.add_grammar t grammar;
    t;;
val tm : TmLanguage.t = <abstr>
]}

With this we can now highlight code blocks that start with [diff].

{@ocaml[
# Hilite.src_code_to_html ~tm ~pre_class:"hilite" ~lang:"diff" "+ hello";;
- : (string, Hilite.error) result =
Ok
 "<pre class='hilite'><code><span class='diff-punctuation-definition-inserted'>+</span><span class='diff-markup-inserted'> hello\n</span></code></pre>"
]}

Note the use of [pre_class] to specifiy a CSS class for the surrounding [pre]
element.
